title AMUS25 — Constel learner: a new member joins non-voting, catches up, then earns the vote
prompt continue the recursion — take the next agent-doable pure crux Lindy-first, self-approve, send as its own signed round; STOP at custody gates
think AMUS24's own log flagged the next AMUS *socket* rung as custody gate #2 (a real file descriptor) — but a socket gate does not gate the whole pure-Raft ladder; a real gap remained upstream
think reconfig (AMUS13) does joint consensus to CHANGE the voting set, and catchup (AMUS15) carries a lagging log forward, yet nothing modeled the safe way to ADD a member — Raft/Ongaro §4.2.1 catching up new servers as a non-voting learner before it counts
think the crux: a fresh member added straight as a voter enlarges the majority it cannot yet answer, so one partitioned old voter stalls commits; staged as a learner the voter majority is unchanged and the sky stays available while it catches up, promoted only once within a bounded lag
think named the module learner.rye — a clean collision-free word; voter_count/voter_majority draw quorum.majority_of over voters only, commit_backing skips a learner's ack, caught_up computes the lag in u64 so a far-ahead leader never wraps, require_caught_up refuses NotCaughtUp
obs GREEN on the first build across six blocks; TAME bans clean, width-check exit 0, zero usize; the crux block proves both paths from one ack set — as-voter stalls (backing 2 of threshold 3), as-learner commits (backing 2 of threshold 2)
obs clock discrepancy noted honestly: the live TZ=America/New_York read is 07:43 EDT, yet the prior AMUS20-24 logs are stamped 08:41-08:49 while git shows AMUS24 committed at 07:33 EDT — the prior session stamped ahead of the true clock; per the never-fabricate rule this rung carries its real live read rather than an invented future stamp, and dated prior logs stay unrewritten (accrete-never-break)
